---
// The release line is read from the package and the changelog, never typed,
// so it cannot drift at the next release (same sources as DeckGallery.astro).
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import Eyebrow from './Eyebrow.astro';

const repoRoot = resolve(process.cwd(), '..');
const pkg = JSON.parse(readFileSync(resolve(repoRoot, 'rikiki/package.json'), 'utf8'));
const version = pkg.version;
const nodeRange = pkg.engines.node;
const changelog = readFileSync(resolve(repoRoot, 'CHANGELOG.md'), 'utf8');
const released = new RegExp(`^## \\[${version}\\] - (\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2})`, 'm').exec(changelog);
if (!released) throw new Error(`Faq · CHANGELOG.md has no dated entry for ${version}`);
const releaseDate = new Date(`${released[1]}T00:00:00Z`).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', {
  day: 'numeric',
  month: 'long',
  year: 'numeric',
  timeZone: 'UTC',
});

const items = [
  {
    q: 'What do I need to know to create a deck?',
    a: 'HTML and CSS are enough. Write the supplied custom elements, then change CSS variables for your brand. Lit, a small Web Components library, is an implementation detail; deck authors do not write Lit classes or templates.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I write my slides in Markdown?',
    a: 'Not as a deck format, and that is a design choice: a slide is an HTML custom element, so the file you write is the file the browser runs, with no custom syntax or <code>---</code> separator to learn. Inside a slide, the core <code>&lt;deck-md&gt;</code> component renders standard GitHub-flavoured Markdown, including headings, lists, links, tables, code blocks and images, plus one <code>::: cards</code> fence that expands into a card grid. A deck can mix HTML slides and Markdown bodies freely.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I print my deck to PDF?',
    a: 'Yes, two ways. From the browser, print with backgrounds on: the deck carries its own print stylesheet, so every slide becomes one page at the deck\u2019s canvas size, chrome hidden. Or run <code>rikiki export deck.html --output deck.pdf</code>, which drives a headless Chromium and waits for fonts and diagrams first. The CLI needs Playwright, an optional peer dependency.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a presenter mode with notes?',
    a: 'Yes. Wrap notes in <code>&lt;deck-notes&gt;</code> inside any slide, then press P during the talk. A popup opens with the current and next slide, the notes, a timer and a slide counter. The two windows synchronize locally through BroadcastChannel; no external service is involved.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I theme a deck for my brand?',
    a: 'Copy <code>themes/rikiki.css</code>, change the palette and semantic CSS variables, then link your file instead of the default theme. For one component instance, override its documented <code>--deck-*</code> variables directly.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I embed a deck in documentation or training?',
    a: 'Yes. Put <code>&lt;deck-root&gt;</code> in a page and add <code>autoplay loop swipe</code> for a touch-friendly carousel with the same ~43 KB initial load. Keyboard input stays scoped to the deck while it has focus.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does rikiki check report?',
    a: '<code>rikiki check</code> loads every slide in Chromium and reports structural, runtime, timing and visual-layout risks. It does not test contrast, focus order or screen-reader output, so keep an accessibility review in your release process.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is a deck accessible?',
    a: 'The example decks are tested with axe in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, and a serious or critical violation fails the suite. The overview opens, moves and picks a slide with the keyboard alone, every on-screen control is a real focusable button, a slide change is announced to assistive technology through a live region, and no slide animates under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code>. <code>rikiki export</code> writes a tagged PDF with one bookmark per slide. Those tests cover the shipped examples and the runtime, not the contrast or the reading order of the content you write.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What requires Node.js?',
    a: `The deck runs in a browser. Node.js <code>${nodeRange}</code> is required for the CLI commands that initialize, inspect, render, bundle or export it. The commands that drive a browser, namely <code>check</code>, <code>render</code> and <code>export</code>, also need the optional Playwright peer dependency: <code>npm i -D playwright &amp;&amp; npx playwright install chromium</code>. <code>bundle</code> and <code>init --standalone</code> need the optional <code>rolldown</code> peer dependency instead.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Which browsers are tested?',
    a: 'The base rendering, navigation, scaling and offline-bundle contract is tested in Chromium, Firefox and WebKit. PDF export and multi-screen presenter features are Chromium-specific.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why is there no build step and no custom syntax?',
    a: 'There is no build step on the author side: the served folder contains the runtime, so you open <code>index.html</code> from any static HTTP server and it runs. Everything rests on Web standards, namely Custom Elements, Shadow DOM, ES Modules and CSS custom properties, with a thin component library rather than an application framework underneath. The source is readable HTML, so a slide reads the same in your editor and in the browser. Features beyond the core, such as presenter mode, transitions or syntax highlighting, are opt-in modules you import only when a deck needs them.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who maintains it, and what is the release status?',
    a: `The project is developed by Cédric Chariere Fiedler and released under the MIT license; source, issues and merge requests live at <a href="https://gitlab.com/tordu-jardin/rikiki">gitlab.com/tordu-jardin/rikiki</a>. Version ${version} shipped on ${releaseDate}, and from that tag a breaking change means a major version bump. The <a href="/docs/changelog">changelog</a> lists every release.`,
  },
];
---

<section id="faq" class="faq">
  <div class="inner">
    <Eyebrow text="FAQ" />
    <h2>Check the requirements before you start.</h2>

    <div class="list">
      {items.map((it) => (
        <details>
          <summary>
            <span class="q">{it.q}</span>
            <span class="chev" aria-hidden="true">+</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="a" set:html={it.a}></div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .faq {
    background: var(--rik-surface-raised);
    padding: var(--rik-space-7) var(--rik-space-5);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rik-border-default);
  }
  .inner { max-width: var(--rik-measure-page); margin: 0 auto; }
  .list { max-width: 880px; }
  h2 {
    font-family: var(--rik-font-display);
    font-size: var(--rik-font-size-mega);
    font-weight: 900;
    color: var(--rik-text-default);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.05;
    margin: 0 0 var(--rik-space-5);
    overflow-wrap:anywhere;
  }
  .list { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  details {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rik-border-default);
    padding: var(--rik-space-3) 0;
  }
  details:last-child { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rik-border-default); }
  summary {
    display: flex; align-items: flex-start; justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--rik-space-3);
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
    padding: var(--rik-space-2) 0;
    color: var(--rik-text-default);
    font-family: var(--rik-font-display);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    line-height: 1.35;
    transition: color var(--rik-motion-fast) var(--rik-motion__ease-out);
  }
  .q { min-width:0; overflow-wrap:anywhere; }
  summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  summary:hover { color: var(--rik-accent--strong); }
  .chev {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 28px; height: 28px;
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    border: 1px solid var(--rik-border-default);
    border-radius: 50%;
    color: var(--rik-text-default--faint);
    font: 700 1.1rem/1 var(--rik-font-mono);
    transition: transform var(--rik-motion-base) var(--rik-motion__ease-out),
                background var(--rik-motion-fast) var(--rik-motion__ease-out),
                color var(--rik-motion-fast) var(--rik-motion__ease-out);
  }
  details[open] .chev {
    transform: rotate(45deg);
    background: var(--rik-accent);
    color: var(--rik-accent__on);
    border-color: var(--rik-accent);
  }
  .a {
    padding: var(--rik-space-2) 0 var(--rik-space-2);
    color: var(--rik-text-default--muted);
    font-size: var(--rik-font-size-body);
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: 70ch;
  }
  .a :global(code) {
    font-family: var(--rik-font-mono);
    background: var(--rik-surface-tint);
    color: var(--rik-text-default);
    padding: 1px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    white-space:normal;
    overflow-wrap:anywhere;
  }
  .a :global(a) {
    color: var(--rik-accent--strong);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  @media (max-width:640px) {
    .faq { padding:var(--rik-space-6) var(--rik-space-3); }
  }
</style>
